{
  "abstract": "We give a positive-source obstruction for Collatz division-exponent words\nwith a bounded number of undetermined positions in periodic skeletons.\nIf at most $m$ positions remain undetermined at arbitrarily large periods,\nthe uniform division mean $\\alpha$ exists. A non-eventually-periodic word\nover a finite positive-integer alphabet has no positive rational realization\nwhen $\\alpha > m\\log_2(3)/(m+1)$. The new range is $\\alpha < \\log_2(3)$.\nThe inverse affine maps of a skeleton have $m+1$ bounded integer features,\neven when the number of its distinct fillings grows exponentially in $m$.\nA weighted telescoping identity removes one feature. Applying the known\nsimultaneous-approximation sign argument to those features gives an explicit\nfinite inequality retaining the original rational source.\n\nAn explicit uncountable family with two separated holes has mean $11/10$.\nWe prove that its asymptotic initial periodic-shadow ratio is exactly $7/5$,\nalso after binary coding, and that every aligned block length at least $80$,\nat every origin, has at least four distinct blocks among its first sixteen.\nThese facts separate the family from the matched density-sensitive shadow\ncriterion and the published aligned-dictionary criteria, including their\nalternative scales. The result is a restriction on prescribed itineraries;\nno argument forces arbitrary integer trajectories to have these skeletons.",
